  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:[-2,2]\\to R$ which is given by $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{3}-x)?$\n\n- $f(-1)=f(1)$\n- $\u2200\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in [-2,2],$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$\n- $f$ is not **one-to-one**",
  "worked_solution": "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14105016/assets/869be2ef6cb317b81c249b85b0afcbea23178dcf3bb77bb008e69375494439df.png)\n\nA function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $\u2200\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have $f(-1)=\\frac{1}{2}((-1)^{3}-(-1))=0$ and $f(1)=\\frac{1}{2}({1}^{3}-1)=0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $f(-1)=f(1)$ but $-1\\ne 1.$ So, it's *not true* that for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $[-2,2],$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since statement II is false, $f$ is not an injection (also known as *one-to-one*).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
